{"name":"Cosmos","hex":"#fcd5cf","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Cosmos","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A taxonomic genus within the family Asteraceae – several American flowering plants."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A city in Minnesota."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Cosmos"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cosmos","blocks":[{"text":"The cosmos is an alternative name for the universe or its nature or order. Usage of the word cosmos implies viewing the universe as a complex and orderly system or entity."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/The_Nights_Long_Moments_%28152181573%29.jpeg/330px-The_Nights_Long_Moments_%28152181573%29.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cosmos"},{"title":"Cosmos (Humboldt book)","blocks":[{"text":"Cosmos: A Sketch of a Physical Description of the Universe is an influential treatise on science and nature written by the German scientist and explorer Alexander von Humboldt. It began as a lecture series delivered by Humboldt at the University of Berlin, and was published in five volumes between 1845 and 1862. In the first volume, Humboldt paints a general \"portrait of nature\", describing the physical nature of outer space and the Earth. In the second volume he describes the history of science."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Stieler%2C_Joseph_Karl_-_Alexander_von_Humboldt_-_1843.jpg/330px-Stieler%2C_Joseph_Karl_-_Alexander_von_Humboldt_-_1843.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cosmos_(Humboldt_book)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Flammarion.jpg/500px-Flammarion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Flammarion.jpg/1